If this body is constructed, the positive-covariant-monodromy theorem places
the spectrum of $Theta-1/2$ on the imaginary axis and proves RH. The first
unsupported construction is no longer “some knot interpretation,” nor a
single map moving through an enlarged total field. It is a common
three-frame incidence carrying the finite-place continuation $A_(S,R)$ and
the cross-frame transports whose returned composition satisfies ("TRACE"),
("COVARIANCE"), and ("SEAM"), with controlled radical, while the same trace
body reproduces ("ORBIT").

= The explicit formula is the population-level bridge

== Fix one sign convention

On $G=RR_(>0)^times$, let $h=f ast f^sharp$ as above. Define the finite-place
face
$
  W_"fin"(h)
  =
  sum_p (log p) sum_(m>=1)
  (h(p^m)+p^(-m)h(p^(-m))).
$
The archimedean face in the convention used by the laboratory record is
$
  W_infinity(h)
  =
  (log(4pi)+gamma_E)h(1)
  +
  integral_1^infinity
  frac(
    h(x)+x^(-1)h(x^(-1))-2x^(-1)h(1),
    x-x^(-1)
  ) dif x.
$
Then put
$
  Q_W(f)
  =
  cal(M)h(0)+cal(M)h(1)-W_"fin"(h)-W_infinity(h).
$
For a standard admissible test class and the prescribed symmetric zero sum,
the completed explicit formula gives
$
  Q_W(f)=sum_rho cal(M)h(rho).
$
Equivalent sources place signs and endpoint terms differently; a proof must
fix one convention and carry every term. The content is invariant: prime
powers plus the infinite place equal the completed zero response
@weil1952 @burnol1998simple @lagarias2013arithmetic.

== Weil positivity is the exact RH closure

On RH, every nontrivial zero has $rho=1/2+i gamma$, so the Mellin-return
lemma gives
$
  cal(M)h(rho)=abs(A_f(i gamma))^2>=0.
$
Conversely, Weil's criterion states—under the full admissible family—that
$
  "RH"
  quad arrow.l.r quad
  Q_W(f)>=0
  quad "for every admissible" f.
$

This is the proof obligation. It is neither a statistical trend nor an
instruction to verify more zeros. It asks for the completed arithmetic
distribution to have no negative direction on the complete adjoint-return
space.

The displayed inequality is written in one fixed chart. Parametrically, it
asks whether the Hermitian section $W_lambda$ is positive semidefinite in
every anchored formulation fiber representing that test space. A family with
$q_lambda(f_lambda)<0$ is entirely possible before RH is known. Finding a
different $g_lambda$ with positive response does not answer it; one must
either control the original direction or transport it covariantly to a
proved-positive base cell.

#proof-spine-figure()

== One exact triangular probe

Let $N>1$, $R=log N$, and take the logarithmic box
$
  a_N(u)=1_([-R/2,R/2])(u).
$
Put
$
  f_N(x)=x^(-1/2)a_N(log x).
$
Then
$
  h_N(x)=x^(-1/2)(R-abs(log x))_+
$
and
$
  A_N(z)=frac(2sinh(z R/2),z).
$
The finite prime face is exactly
$
  W_"fin"(h_N)
  =
  2sum_(p^m<N)(log p)p^(-m/2)(R-m log p).
$
On the seam,
$
  A_N(i gamma)=frac(2sin(gamma R/2),gamma).
$

This is a useful geometric tent: support in log scale becomes a triangular
return, and only finitely many prime powers cross it. It is not smooth at the
boundary and the one-parameter family is not the full Weil test family.
Passing every such finite probe would not by itself prove RH.

