The analogy is now subordinate to exact operators. No electrical physics is
being asserted.

== The proof-bearing object is the uniform form-level successor

#object-entry(elements.receiver_domination_rh)

The previous laboratory record phrased the gap as the scalar domination
$
  Q_W(f)>=Sigma_(S_f)(f)>=0
$
for every admissible $f$ in a sufficiently complete contemporary receiver.
That remains one sufficient local chart, but it is not the only geometrically
possible proof form.

In that chart, a local prime-admission step would require
$
  D_S(f)
  >=
  W_p(f ast f^sharp)+kappa_(S,p)(f)
$
whenever $p$ enters. No term on the right is individually required to be
positive. No theorem audited here proves this inequality.

#object-entry(elements.transported_positive_atlas_rh)

This corollary remains a valid global reformulation. It permits
many nonidentical formulations, curved parameter trajectories, and changing
receivers. It does not demand that every local contribution or every
intermediate evaluation be positive. It demands four relational invariants:

1. *coverage:* every admissible returned current occurs in the atlas;
2. *anchoring:* transition maps carry the same completed explicit-formula
   occurrence rather than merely reaching an equal endpoint;
3. *positive covariance:* the Hermitian response changes only by a positive
   congruence, or by an explicitly controlled nonnegative defect; and
4. *coherent return:* composition and holonomy cannot carry a positive cone
   into a negative one.

This is not a vague enlarged total field, and it is not the method required
for the proof. Every compact current belongs to one finite support stage.
The induction theorem above is sufficient; an atlas is a way to organize its
coherence under alternate prime orders and receiver recharts. Its
local comparisons may be spans or partial maps; only invertible exact
recharts form a groupoid. The global obligation is that these incidences and
their transition arcs cover the complete admissible family without losing the
response. A single positive orientation in each neighborhood is insufficient;
a complete anchored correspondence atlas whose closed returns preserve
the response would be sufficient.

#object-entry(elements.squeezed_positive_return_rh)

The Squeeze Theorem supplies a third exact proof form. For every admissible
returned current, it would suffice to construct independently positive
receiver responses $Sigma_n(f)$ and complete errors $E_n(f)$ satisfying
$
  abs(Q_W(f)-Sigma_n(f))<=E_n(f),
  quad
  E_n(f) arrow.r 0.
$
Then $Q_W(f)>=-E_n(f)$ for every $n$, so $Q_W(f)>=0$ in the limit.

This does not authorize an infinite prime sweep. A compact current already
meets only finitely many finite places. The useful approximation index would
refine the final receiver—through prolate/Sonin resolution, aperture,
certified Galerkin transport, or completed formulation charts—and its error
must retain endpoint, archimedean, finite-place, aperture-connection, and
holonomy terms. No such complete error squeeze is currently derived.

== The error has a vanishing resolution face and a persistent structural face

#object-entry(elements.nested_aperture_deficit_persistence)

This rules out one tempting but false use of the Squeeze Theorem.  If an
admissible compact current has negative completed response at aperture $R$,
the same current and response remain present at every larger compatible
aperture.  Enlarging support cannot dilute that occurrence.  In the
finite-aperture spectral notation, the lower floors therefore satisfy
$
  mu_(R')<=mu_R
  quad "when" R'>=R.
$
The associated deficit $(-mu_R)_+$ can only stay fixed or grow.  It is not a
vanishing squeeze parameter.

Now hold the final receiver $(S,R)$ fixed and resolve only its positive Sonin
return.  Put
$
  A_S(f)=Theta_S(f)P_S.
$
For increasing finite-rank output projections $L_n arrow.r I$, define
$
  Sigma_(S,n)(f)=norm(L_n A_S(f))_"HS"^2.
$
Then the exact Hilbert--Schmidt tail is
$
  T_(S,n)(f)
  =
  norm((I-L_n)A_S(f))_"HS"^2
  arrow.r 0
$
and
$
  Q_W(f)-Sigma_(S,n)(f)
  =
  D_S(f)+T_(S,n)(f).
$
Thus receiver resolution converges to $Sigma_S$, not automatically to $Q_W$.
The vanishing tail exposes the structural defect; it does not erase it.

#object-entry(elements.positive_remainder_resolution_squeeze)

This theorem corrects the previous description of the missing object.  The
complete error $E_n$ is automatic after one constructs a positive remainder
carrier
$
  D_S(f)=norm(C_(S,R)f)^2.
$
The genuinely proof-bearing object is $C_(S,R)$, or equivalently a
nonnegative remainder form $K_(S,R)$.  Under one prime admission its exact
law is
$
  K_(S union {p},R)
  =
  K_(S,R)-C_(S,p),
$
where
$
  C_(S,p)(f)
  =
  W_p(f ast f^sharp)+kappa_(S,p)(f).
$
Consequently the archimedean positive base propagates precisely when
$
  C_(S,p)<=K_(S,R)
$
in Hermitian-form order.  This is the same unproved semilocal domination
relation, now separated from all discretization tails.  It does not say that
$C_(S,p)$ is positive.  A covariant carrier may remain a norm square at both
receivers while this signed difference points in either direction.

== One finite error squeeze is already available

#object-entry(elements.finite_archimedean_tail_squeeze)

The finite Guinand--Weil tail theorem supplies, on each fixed finite
frequency band, an exact positive archimedean increment
$
  0<=Q_infinity-Q_T<=B_T I,
  quad
  B_T arrow.r 0.
$
Once the finite shifted matrix $Q_T+B_T I$ is certified positive, it is an
independently nonnegative approximant and $B_T norm(v)^2$ is the requested
vanishing error.  This genuinely closes the archimedean integration-cutoff
axis.  What it does not close is the frequency-band limit, support growth, or
the prime-induced remainder form.

= The remainder carrier as a covariant coarea sweep

The phrase “positive remainder carrier” should not be read as an instruction
to append a positive block to the receiver. A carrier is a contemporary
geometric interior: a measured field of response amplitudes whose squared
norm is read at one receiver. When that receiver changes, its complete norm
remains nonnegative while its derivative and seam jumps may be signed.

This distinction is forced by the defect recurrence. The local change
$
  -W_p(f ast f^sharp)-kappa_(S,p)(f)
$
has no required sign. Demanding a positive constituent from every prime would
replace the established receiver rebase by a false monotonicity law.

== Change of variables carries a half-Jacobian

#object-entry(elements.covariant_coarea_carrier)

Let $Phi:U arrow.r V$ be a regular coordinate change. If $b(x)$ is a carrier
amplitude on $V$, then the amplitude representing the same quadratic response
on $U$ is
$
  tilde(b)(u)
  =
  abs(det D Phi(u))^(1/2)R_u b(Phi(u)),
$
where $R_u$ carries the output fiber unitarily. Consequently
$
  integral_V norm(b(x))^2 dif x
  =
  integral_U norm(tilde(b)(u))^2 dif u.
$
The Jacobian belongs to amplitude before the square is evaluated. It is not a
scalar correction pasted onto a completed answer.

#object-entry(elements.covariant_coarea_fundamental_theorem)

For a carrier section $Psi(t)=C_(rho(t))f_t$ along a path of contemporary
receivers, the exact covariant FTC is
$
  norm(Psi(b))^2-norm(Psi(a))^2
  =
  2 Re sum_j integral_(I_j)
  chevron.l Psi,nabla_(partial_t)Psi chevron.r dif t
  +
  sum_k
  (
    norm(Psi(t_k^+))^2-norm(Psi(t_k^-))^2
  ).
$
Every endpoint norm is nonnegative. Neither the connection density nor a
discrete seam difference is required to be nonnegative. This is the precise
calculus relation between a positive interior and a signed loss/current
signal.

#coarea-carrier-figure()

== Cartesian and polar coordinates give the finite-dimensional control

For
$
  x=r cos theta,
  quad
  y=r sin theta,
$
one has
$
  dif x dif y=r dif r dif theta
$
and therefore
$
  integral_(RR^2) abs(f(x,y))^2 dif x dif y
  =
  integral_0^infinity integral_0^Theta
  abs(f(r,theta))^2 r dif theta dif r.
$
The amplitude chart carries $r^(1/2)$. If
$
  f(r,theta)=sum_(n in ZZ) f_n(r)e^(i n theta),
$
then angular Parseval gives
$
  norm(f)^2
  =
  norm(f_0)^2
  +
  sum_(n!=0)norm(f_n)^2.
$
Reading only the rotationally symmetric face leaves the nonzero winding modes
as a literal positive remainder carrier.

This example also displays the required seams. Away from $r=0$ and after
choosing an angular branch, the chart is locally reversible. Complete-turn
lifts can present the same Cartesian point with different winding. At $r=0$,
the Jacobian loses rank and every angular direction collapses to one point.
That critical locus cannot be handled by silently setting the regular fiber
density to zero.

The radial FTC
$
  V(R)
  =
  integral_0^R integral_0^Theta r dif theta dif r,
  quad
  V'(R)=Theta R
$
shows why a boundary face alone is insufficient. The disk is carried by the
ordered family of circular fibers, their normal direction, induced measure,
and sweep.

== Logarithmic transport makes the critical half-density exact

#object-entry(elements.mellin_half_density_chart)

#object-entry(elements.weighted_mellin_basis_rebase)

With $r=e^u$, define
$
  (cal(U)F)(u)=e^(u/2)F(e^u).
$
Then
$
  integral_0^infinity abs(F(r))^2 dif r
  =
  integral_(-infinity)^infinity
  abs((cal(U)F)(u))^2 dif u
$
and ordinary Fourier transformation gives
$
  hat(cal(U)F)(t)
  =
  cal(M)F(1/2-i t).
$
Thus the critical half has an exact change-of-variable provenance: it is the
square-root Jacobian carrying the additive $L^2(dif r)$ response into
logarithmic scale. On multiplicative Haar measure $dif r/r$, the logarithmic
chart already has unit Jacobian; the half-density transports between these
two normalizations.

More generally, for
$
  dif mu_beta(r)=r^beta frac(dif r,r),
$
the transported amplitude is $e^(beta u/2)F(e^u)$ and Fourier transformation
lands on the Mellin line $op("Re")(s)=beta/2$. Reciprocal conjugation becomes
$J_beta(s)=beta-overline(s)$. Moving from $beta$ to $beta'$ while carrying
the function and measure together conjugates this fixed seam; changing only
one of them changes the problem. The completed zeta relation used here is the
$beta=1$ member. Its displayed $1/2$ therefore belongs to this weighted
involution and half-density, not to base ten and not to the $1/q$ of a finite
character projector.

Under
$
  F^sharp(r)=r^(-1)overline(F(r^(-1))),
$
the same chart gives
$
  cal(U)(F^sharp)(u)
  =
  overline((cal(U)F)(-u)).
$
Scale inversion has become reflection of logarithmic position, and the
Mellin fixed seam is exactly where the returned pair is a norm square.

== Hyperbolic coordinates separate common energy from oriented residual

The Riemannian hyperbolic plane has
$
  dif s^2=dif rho^2+sinh^2(rho)dif theta^2,
  quad
  dif A=sinh(rho)dif rho dif theta.
$
Its radial sweep obeys
$
  A(R)=Theta sinh R,
  quad
  V(R)=Theta(cosh R-1),
  quad
  V'(R)=A(R).
$
This is a positive coarea geometry: $sinh rho$ records the expansion of
successive boundary fibers.

Lorentzian hyperbolic coordinates carry a different relation:
$
  X=rho cosh eta,
  quad
  T=rho sinh eta,
$
$
  dif X^2-dif T^2
  =
  dif rho^2-rho^2 dif eta^2,
  quad
  X plus.minus T=rho e^(plus.minus eta).
$
The reciprocal factors $e^(plus.minus eta)$ are the same hyperbolic species
as the centered prime sheets $e^(plus.minus x-i theta)$. Their common metric
face is proportional to $cosh(2x)$ and is positive; their oriented
half-difference is proportional to $sinh(2x)$ and changes sign across the
fixed seam. The Lorentzian difference records normal hand. It is not itself
a positive carrier.

This supplies the correct division:
$
  "positive common/Gram interior"
  quad plus quad
  "signed hyperbolic normal and connection current".
$

== The Euler metric supplies an operator half-Jacobian

#object-entry(elements.metric_half_jacobian_aperture)

For one receiver transition $J$, put $G=J^*J$. Its polar decomposition is
$
  J=U G^(1/2),
$
with $U$ unitary. Hence
$
  norm(J v)^2=norm(G^(1/2)v)^2.
$
The positive stretch $G^(1/2)$ is the operator analogue of the scalar
half-Jacobian.

If $Pi$ is the pulled-back successor projection, which is orthogonal in the
$G$-metric, then
$
  tilde(P)=G^(1/2)Pi G^(-1/2)
$
is an ordinary orthogonal projection. Pythagoras now gives the exact successor
decomposition
$
  norm(J v)^2
  =
  norm(G^(1/2)Pi v)^2
  +
  norm(G^(1/2)(I-Pi)v)^2.
$
The complement is genuinely positive, but it belongs to the complete
successor metric. It is neither an untouched predecessor block nor, without
another identity, the completed Weil remainder.

== The archimedean remainder already has a real amplitude

#object-entry(elements.archimedean_remainder_amplitude)

This improves the status of the carrier problem. At the one-place aperture,
the remainder is not merely known to be nonnegative. Connes and Consani
construct the operator producing it:
$
  N_2=-2epsilon'(1^+)(I-K_2),
$
where $K_2$ comes from the prolate/Sonin kernel $Q epsilon$. After the
constant mode is removed, $-N_2$ is positive. Its spectral square root
therefore gives the exact amplitude
$
  A_2=(-P_0N_2P_0)^(1/2)
$
and, on the source's conditioned current family,
$
  W_infinity(g ast g^*)-Sigma_infinity(g)
  =
  norm(A_2 xi_g)^2.
$

This square root is not circular: $N_2$ was constructed from the
archimedean trace remainder before the inequality was read. Its spectral
resolution is an actual direct-integral carrier field. The open problem has
therefore moved from “find any arithmetic amplitude” to “continue this
amplitude through support growth and finite-place rebase.”

== Prime powers enter as translated aperture intersections

#object-entry(elements.prime_power_aperture_incidence)

For a logarithmic current $a$ supported on an interval of length $log R$,
the finite-place response is exactly
$
  W_p(h)
  =
  2log p sum_(m>=1)p^(-m/2)
  Re chevron.l a,U_(m log p)a chevron.r.
$
Each term lives on the overlap between the aperture and its translate by
$m log p$. It is present precisely when $p^m<R$. For the box current, the
overlap length is $log R-m log p$, recovering the earlier exact
prime-population formula without treating the support cutoff as an external
enumeration rule.

#object-entry(elements.prime_power_hinge_cell_recurrence)

This closes the combinatorial scaling question.  In the normalized aperture
square, every active $p^k$ cuts a pair of affine triangular hinge cells.
Passing from $I_n$ to $I_(n+1)$ changes the cell incidence exactly when $n$
is a prime power; otherwise the same cells deform.  The number of traversals
on one prime axis at the successor endpoint is
$
  floor(frac(log n,log p)).
$

The corresponding form is not a sum of positive prime tiles.  Each hinge is
exactly a weighted squared translation difference minus its diagonal
potential.  More importantly, the conditioned short subtracts the squared
norm of the *sum* of all old-energy carriers.  Its expansion therefore
contains cross-terms between distinct prime powers and the archimedean face.
The raw threshold lines are parallel; their consequential intersections are
created by their common old carrier.

== The Euler resolvent folds the complete prime tower into one return

#object-entry(elements.normalized_euler_resolvent_return)

Put $a=p^(-1/2)$ and $J_p=I-a U_(log p)$. The normalized inverse
$
  cal(R)_p
  =
  sqrt(1-p^(-1))J_p^(-1)
$
has the exact Poisson-kernel metric
$
  cal(R)_p^*cal(R)_p
  =
  I+sum_(m>=1)p^(-m/2)
  (U_(m log p)+U_(m log p)^*).
$
Consequently the *whole* prime-power response, including every translated
overlap which the current can meet, is one endpoint norm change:
$
  W_p(f ast f^sharp)
  =
  log p
  (
    norm(cal(R)_p L_R f)^2
    -
    norm(L_R f)^2
  ).
$
The signed prime current is therefore not a detached negative or positive
block. It is the difference between two positive amplitudes before and
after one returned Euler passage.

The same operator is already present in the global arithmetic population.
If
$
  (cal(E)_S f)(x)
  =
  abs(x)^(1/2)
  sum_(m in cal(M)_S) f(m x),
$
where $cal(M)_S$ contains the positive integers prime to the finite places
of $S$, then partitioning that population into terms divisible and not
divisible by a new prime gives
$
  cal(E)_(S union {p})
  =
  J_p cal(E)_S,
  quad
  cal(R)_p cal(E)_(S union {p})
  =
  sqrt(1-p^(-1))cal(E)_S.
$
Prime admission removes the $p$-divisible sheet; the resolvent returns the
predecessor population from the admitted one. The powers $p^m$ are the
successive feedback turns of this single relation. The associated two-port
Julia operator is unitary, so the return can be embedded in a lossless
colligation even though its observed prime channel is not contractive.

#object-entry(elements.archimedean_carrier_prime_boundary)

The published base interval has length $log 2$. Every prime translation is
at least $log 2$, so its interior overlap is empty. This is why the
archimedean theorem excludes finite primes geometrically, and why applying
the Euler difference while compressing back into the same base interval
cannot propagate the remainder: the translated arm is erased before it can
meet the current.

The needed successor is therefore not a conjugate of one fixed $N_2$. It is
an expanded, receiver-relative operator $B_(S,R)$ whose polarized form obeys
$
  B_(S union {p},R)-B_(S,R)
  =
  -cal(W)_(p,R)-cal(K)_(S,p,R).
$
The first term is now the explicit translated-overlap form; the second is
the already-derived Sonin-aperture connection. The semilocal sources
construct the receiving space and its Sonin transport, but do not construct
this remainder operator or prove this equality.

== The first dyadic contact has two polarizations

#object-entry(elements.dyadic_euler_boundary_polarization)

The smallest joint support/place cell is now explicit. Set
$ell=log 2$ and enlarge the logarithmic aperture from
$
  I_2=[-ell/2,ell/2]
$
to
$
  I_4=[-ell,ell].
$
“Joint” here means that one fixed logarithmic receiver admits both the first
support growth and the first prime translation. It does not mean that the
arithmetic, archimedean, and receiving lineages have already been constructed
as one asynchronous triangle. The ambient $L^2$ fiber, translation, aperture,
and inner product remain fixed for this local calculation.

Dividing $I_4$ into four consecutive half-$ell$ cells turns translation by
$ell$ into two incidence chains:
$
  e_3 arrow.r e_1 arrow.r "outside",
  quad
  e_4 arrow.r e_2 arrow.r "outside".
$
Thus the compressed translation has square zero. Because the Euler weight and
the lossless-port normalization agree at the first prime,
$
  a^2=2^(-1)=1-a^2,
$
the normalized compressed return has a positive rank-two defect $B_-^*B_-$.
The discarded continuation is not nothing: its exact Gram operator is the
second positive rank-two channel $B_+^*B_+$. Their difference is the full
prime face:
$
  -W_2(h)
  =
  ell(
    norm(B_-x)^2-norm(B_+x)^2
  ).
$

This is the first concrete cross-face which the earlier scalar recurrence
hid. The minus and plus channels are not separate facts about the prime.
They are the two polarizations of the same translated core/shell incidence:
$
  abs(a u+a^2v)^2-abs(a u-a^2v)^2
  =
  4a^3 op("Re")(overline(u)v).
$
The finite-prime term is therefore an interference or cross term between
two occurrences of one current, not a scalar property attached to either
occurrence.

#object-entry(elements.dyadic_character_filler_bound)

The radix/character distinction makes this boundary sharper. The core--shell
exchange
$
  R=T+T^*
$
is an exact $C_2$ action. Its symmetric and antisymmetric projectors are
$
  P_+=(I+R)/2,
  quad
  P_-=(I-R)/2.
$
The contribution of $-W_2$ to the completed defect is
$
  cal(D)_2
  =
  frac(log 2,sqrt(2))(P_--P_+).
$
Thus the missing passage does not merely owe “some positivity.” After
anchoring to this cell, its predecessor-remainder and aperture contribution
$F_4$ must satisfy
$
  P_+F_4P_+
  >=
  frac(log 2,sqrt(2))P_+.
$
If the passage mixes character modes, it also owes the exact Schur
complement recorded in the theorem. A positive filler which repairs only the
isolated prime contribution has unique minimum trace at
$
  H_min=frac(log 2,sqrt(2))P_+.
$
This does not construct the semilocal filler, but it rules out every
candidate which misses the symmetric core--shell modes or treats the four
cells independently.

== The positive half is an oriented boundary factor

#object-entry(elements.oriented_half_density_crossing)

The symbol $L_+$ had been used too loosely in the preceding discussion.
Hereafter the laboratory crossing operator is written $cal(L)_+$ to keep it
separate from the positive crossing diagram in a knot skein triple. In the
logarithmic coordinate it is
$
  cal(L)_+=partial_u+1/2,
  quad
  cal(L)_-=-partial_u+1/2,
$
and the differential operator which imposes the two Mellin vanishing
characters factors exactly as
$
  -partial_u^2+1/4
  =
  cal(L)_- cal(L)_+
  =
  cal(L)_+^*cal(L)_+.
$
This is the same support-preserving operator used by Connes and Consani to
describe the ideal of test functions vanishing at the two half-characters
@connesconsani2020weil.

The factorization gives the positive $1/2$ an ontological role which a sign
label does not. On a finite oriented interval,
$
  norm(cal(L)_+ h)^2
  =
  norm(partial_u h)^2+1/4 norm(h)^2
  +
  1/2(
    abs(h(b))^2-abs(h(a))^2
  ).
$
The interior closes positively; the direction of crossing remains visible as
one signed boundary flux. Reversing the receiver reverses this boundary term
without changing the closed second-order interior.

The same factor commutes with positive convolution closure:
$
  (-partial_u^2+1/4)(g ast g^*)
  =
  (cal(L)_+ g) ast (cal(L)_+ g)^*.
$
Thus $1/2$ is not inserted after the fact to repair a sign. It is the
first-order connection coefficient whose pairing with the opposite
orientation creates the positive interior. It simultaneously halves
logarithmic support:
$
  op("supp")(g) subset [-A/2,A/2]
  quad arrow.r.double quad
  op("supp")(g ast g^*) subset [-A,A].
$
For $A=log 2$, exponentiation gives the exact passage
$
  [2^(-1/2),2^(1/2)]
  arrow.r
  [1/2,2]
$
used in the published archimedean positive cell. The coefficient $1/2$, the
square-root support endpoints, and the critical Mellin chart are therefore
three faces of one amplitude/adjoint closure, not three coincident glyphs.

== Convexity turns the half into a crossing surface

#object-entry(elements.convex_half_overlap_crossing)

The geometric face is supplied by an autocorrelation rather than by drawing a
preferred contour. For a convex body $K$, define
$
  c_K(x)
  =
  op("Vol")(K inter (K+x))
  =
  (1_K ast 1_K^*)(x).
$
This is a genuine field of co-present interior: its value measures what
remains common while one boundary is transported relative to the other. Its
Fourier transform is $abs(hat(1_K))^2$, and its support is the difference
body $K-K$; these are the standard covariogram relations
@bianchi2016covariogram. Brunn--Minkowski makes
$c_K^(1/n)$ concave @gardner2002brunnminkowski. Consequently every
normalized overlap stratum
$
  Omega_t={x:c_K(x)>=t c_K(0)}
$
is convex and centrally symmetric.

The positively oriented half crossing is now an actual receiver-relative
surface:
$
  L_+(K)
  =
  partial Omega_(1/2)
  =
  {x:c_K(x)=c_K(0)/2}.
$
The opposite receiver orientation sees $L_-(K)$. Neighboring level surfaces
carry the change of interior by coarea:
$
  op("Vol")(Omega_alpha)-op("Vol")(Omega_beta)
  =
  integral_alpha^beta
  integral_(c_K^(-1)(t c_K(0)))
  frac(c_K(0),norm(nabla c_K))
  dif cal(H)^(n-1) dif t.
$
This is the missing change-of-boundary calculus. Curvature is present in the
rate at which the level surface sweeps volume, through both its area measure
and the normal derivative of $c_K$.

The literal half must be stated at the correct type. The raw,
dimension-carrying Hausdorff area of $L_+(K)$ is not universally $1/2$.
What is exactly $1/2$ is the normalized co-present interior on that boundary.
For the logarithmic interval $K=[-ell/2,ell/2]$,
$
  c_K(x)=(ell-abs(x))_+,
$
so the two oriented crossings occur at $x=plus.minus ell/2$ and each retains
exactly half of the original interval. At $ell=log 2$, those points exponentiate
to $2^(plus.minus 1/2)$. This realizes the user's proposed “surface equal to
one half” as a half-overlap boundary, while coarea records the actual surface
measure which transports one neighboring shape into the next.

== Dyadic Poisson return fills the local positive half

#object-entry(elements.dyadic_poisson_half_filler)

The four-cell defect no longer stops at an abstract minimum projector. Let
$Pi_ell$ periodize the logarithmic current over the lattice
$ell ZZ$. On the established dyadic basis it identifies precisely the two
translated pairs:
$
  Pi_ell e_1=Pi_ell e_3,
  quad
  Pi_ell e_2=Pi_ell e_4.
$
Therefore
$
  Pi_ell^*Pi_ell=2P_+,
  quad
  overline(Pi)_ell=Pi_ell/sqrt(2),
  quad
  overline(Pi)_ell^*overline(Pi)_ell=P_+.
$
This gives the symmetric character projector a geometric meaning. It is the
part of the four-cell current which descends to the logarithmic quotient; the
antisymmetric part is the orientation-sensitive fiber killed by
periodization. Its rank is two in a four-dimensional cell, hence its
normalized operator area is exactly $1/2$.

The scale is not fitted from a numerical deficit. The global scaling map has
dyadic arm
$
  e^(u/2)f(2e^u)
  =
  2^(-1/2)h(u+log 2),
$
and the Euler normal derivative contributes $log 2$. Thus
$
  c=(log 2)2^(-1/2)=frac(log 2,sqrt(2))
$
is fixed before the quotient is evaluated. The normalized Poisson amplitude
$
  A_("Pois",2)=sqrt(c)overline(Pi)_ell
$
then satisfies
$
  A_("Pois",2)^*A_("Pois",2)
  =
  c P_+
  =
  H_min.
$
It cancels exactly the negative symmetric face:
$
  cal(D)_2+A_("Pois",2)^*A_("Pois",2)
  =
  c P_- >=0.
$
This closes the isolated dyadic minimum-filler problem by an actual
periodization map. It is a construction, not a restatement of the required
inequality.

The earlier draft drew the wrong conclusion from this cell. Poisson summation
does give
$
  cal(E)(cal(F)f)=cal(I)cal(E)(f)
$
on the two-vanishing-condition Schwartz domain
@connesconsani2019scaling, and the semilocal Sonin maps are Hilbertian
isomorphisms on every aperture
@connesconsanimoscovici2023prolate. But
$A_("Pois",2)$ cannot be a restriction of that successor. The successor and
its dual return are invertible; the periodization amplitude kills the entire
antisymmetric space $P_-cal(V)_4$.

The quotient is instead an exact spectral face of the admission metric. With
$a=2^(-1/2)$,
$
  G_2=(1+a^2)I-a R
$
and therefore
$
  A_("Pois",2)^*A_("Pois",2)
  =
  frac(log 2,2)
  ((1+a+a^2)I-G_2).
$
It records the lower-metric symmetric defect of the dyadic contact, not the
whole transition.

The remaining object is consequently form-level: when support grows, couple
the already-positive old body to the newly admitted shell, identify the
actual cross term, and construct the positive part of the shell left after
that cross term is shorted through the old carrier. The dyadic quotient is
one required face of this successor, not the successor map itself.

== The proof architecture is ordinary mathematical induction

#object-entry(elements.conditioned_support_quotient_shorting)

The source imposes its two conditions on the amplitude:
$
  hat(g)(0)=0,
  quad
  hat(g)(sigma i/2)=0.
$
The operator
$
  cal(Q)=-(rho partial_rho)^2+frac(1,4)
$
belongs to a different face: it imposes the two half-character zeros on the
returned convolution. Treating $cal(Q)C_c^infinity(I_n)$ as the canonical
amplitude space had silently mixed those faces @connesconsani2020weil.

In the logarithmic chart, the corrected Hilbert amplitude fiber is the
codimension-two moment kernel
$
  cal(T)_n
  =
  op("ker")[
    g mapsto
    (
      integral_(I_n)g(u)dif u,
      integral_(I_n)e^(sigma u/2)g(u)dif u
    )
  ],
  quad
  I_n=(-log(n)/2,log(n)/2).
$
Its returned current is supported in $[n^(-1),n]$. Define $P(n)$ to mean
that the completed Weil form is nonnegative on the whole conditioned fiber.
The published archimedean theorem supplies $P(2)$.

Moment conditioning changes the geometry of support growth. With
$I_3=I_2 union S$, let $M_o,M_s$ be the old and shell moment maps and put
$
  L=-M_o^*(M_o M_o^*)^(-1) M_s,
  quad
  J y=(L y)⊕y.
$
Then
$
  cal(T)_3=cal(T)_2⊕J L^2(S),
  quad
  J^*J=I+M_s^*(M_o M_o^*)^(-1) M_s.
  quad "(CONDITIONED QUOTIENT)"
$
The sum is orthogonal in the ambient $L^2$ receiver, but $J y$ is not a
detached shell: its old component is exactly what preserves both moments.

Consequently a raw block
$
  K=mat(A,&B;B^*,&D)
$
becomes, in conditioned coordinates,
$
  H_2=P_o A|_(cal(T)_2),
  quad
  C=P_o(A L+B),
$
$
  D_"cond"=L^* A L+L^* B+B^* L+D.
  quad "(RECHART)"
$
This is why the quotient metric and the crossings must be derived together,
rather than declaring a shell and later imposing constraints.

#object-entry(elements.weil_support_induction_reduction)

The proof still has the form of ordinary induction, but its source-native
statement is a shorting theorem for closed forms, not a bounded
Moore--Penrose calculation. Assume $P(n)$, quotient the radical of $q_n$,
and complete in the $q_n$-norm to obtain $cal(E)_n$. Choose a form-domain
lift $tilde(J)_n$ of the Hilbert quotient. For a quotient direction $y$, the
cross functional
$
  ell_(n,y)(x)=q_(n+1)(x,tilde(J)_n y)
$
must descend to a continuous functional on $cal(E)_n$. If its Riesz carrier
is $c_(n,y)$, the exact remaining face is
$
  s_n(y)
  =
  q_(n+1)(tilde(J)_n y)-norm(c_(n,y))^2.
  quad "(FORM SHORT)"
$
Then $P(n) arrow.r P(n+1)$ holds exactly when every cross functional
descends and $s_n>=0$. In a bounded chart this becomes
$
  op("Ran")(C_n) subset op("Ran")(H_n^(1/2)),
  quad
  D_n-Y_n^*Y_n>=0,
  quad
  C_n=H_n^(1/2)Y_n.
  quad "(INDUCTION)"
$
Douglas factorization rewrites the first clause as
$C_n C_n^*<=c_n H_n$ for some finite $c_n$. Merely annihilating
$op("ker")(H_n)$ is weaker when the square-root range is not closed.
This matters here: the localized Weil form is naturally a closed
lower-semibounded form with an unbounded Friedrichs operator, while its
derivative-current representation is a compact continuous-kernel operator
@suzuki2026screw.

Every step adds support. If $n$ is prime, the exact dual
$Delta_n/(Delta_n^*)^(-1)$ pair is also admitted. If $n$ is a higher prime
power, the shell exposes another traversal of a place already present.
Thus prime powers do not require a separate induction principle.

The interval receiver also locates the positive half precisely. A prime
shift by $log p$ occupies the normalized overlap
$
  omega_p(R)
  =
  frac((log R-log p)_+,log R),
$
so $omega_p(p^2)=1/2$. The $L_+$ half-overlap is therefore a canonical
midpoint of the support continuation of the $p$-arm, not the instant at
which the place first enters. For $p=2$, this is exactly the passage from
$R=2$ to the dyadic $R=4$ contact.

That dyadic contact is not the first induction step. The actual base
successor is $P(2) arrow.r P(3)$, where $p=2$ enters with overlap
$
  log(3/2),
  quad
  omega_2(3)=frac(log(3/2),log 3).
$
At $R=4$, the prime $3$ has also entered the integer filtration. The
isolated $p=2$, $R=4$ character block is therefore a valuable calibration
face, but it is not the complete $P(4)$ receiver.

#object-entry(elements.prime_admission_cross_shell)

The first prime arm can now be located in the *raw* support split exactly.
At $P(p) arrow.r P(p+1)$, translation by $log p$ is the full width of the
old aperture. Consequently
$
  P_"old" U_p P_"old"=0,
  quad
  P_"shell" U_p P_"shell"=0.
$
The left shell translates into a right old boundary strip, while the
opposite old boundary strip translates into the right shell. Both have width
$
  frac(1,2)log frac(p+1,p).
$
Because $p^2>p+1$, no repeated traversal is present yet. The newly admitted
prime has zero diagonal in the unconditioned $L^2(I_p)⊕L^2(S)$ split. After
conditioning, ("RECHART") gives
$
  C_p=P_o B_p,
  quad
  D_(p,"cond")=L^* B_p+B_p^* L.
$
The previous claim that the prime remained “only cross” in the actual
successor was therefore false: the old compensator returns that incidence
into the conditioned shell diagonal. This does not make the prime an
independent positive constituent. Its returned diagonal and the
archimedean/Sonin response must close one joint shorted form.

Once ("INDUCTION") is constructed uniformly, ordinary induction proves every
$P(n)$. Every compact admissible current lies in some $cal(T)_n$, so Weil
positivity and RH follow. “Every support” is the consequence of compactness
and induction, not a demand for exhaustive search.

== The successor proof is one cross-channel contraction

#object-entry(elements.semilocal_successor_defect_contraction)

The turned Sonin aperture also has an endpoint-amplitude expression. With
$
  tilde(P)_p
  =
  G_p^(1/2)Pi_p G_p^(-1/2)
$
orthogonal in the fixed chart,
$
  kappa_(S,p)(f)
  =
  norm(Theta_S(f)tilde(P)_p)_"HS"^2
  -
  norm(Theta_S(f)P_S)_"HS"^2.
$
Assume the predecessor remainder has already been constructed as
$D_S(f)=norm(C_(S,R)f)^2$. Collect its three outgoing amplitudes and the
two successor amplitudes as
$
  X f
  =
  (
    C_(S,R)f,
    sqrt(log p)L_R f,
    Theta_S(f)P_S
  ),
$
$
  Y f
  =
  (
    sqrt(log p)cal(R)_p L_R f,
    Theta_S(f)tilde(P)_p
  ).
$
The completed recurrence is now exactly
$
  D_(S union {p})(f)
  =
  norm(X f)^2-norm(Y f)^2.
$
Thus the successor is positive for every admitted current exactly when the
rule
$
  Gamma_(S,p,R)(X f)=Y f
$
extends to a contraction. If that contraction is independently constructed,
the successor amplitude is not guessed:
$
  C_(S union {p},R)
  =
  (I-Gamma_(S,p,R)^*Gamma_(S,p,R))^(1/2)X.
$

This is the fixed-support, prime-admission face of the induction step.
Constructing $Gamma$ after assuming the inequality would be circular. It
also cannot be a
block-diagonal collection of the known channels: $cal(R)_p$ expands some
translation modes, while the aperture connection can have either sign.
The required contraction must mix the predecessor remainder, prime return,
and Sonin aperture through one arithmetic relation.

The global Poisson formula is the natural source of that mixing because it
joins the semilocal Euler population to additive Fourier return,
multiplicative inversion, and the common principal-value normalization
@connesconsani2019scaling. The elementary admission identity above supplies
its Euler arm; it does not yet supply the Fourier/Sonin off-diagonal blocks.

== Form-level coherence remains a naturality condition

The local factorization is an instance of Douglas' operator
majorization/factorization principle @douglas1966. Category theory sharpens
the coherence of the induction without changing its sign obligation. Let
$cal(J)$ have the actual support inclusions, prime admissions,
Poisson--Sonin returns, and anchored receiver recharts as arrows. The
published Sonin spaces and prime maps already supply the underlying
space-level functor. The completed forms and amplitude sides must respect it:
$
  cal(T),cal(K)_X,cal(K)_Y:cal(J) arrow.r op("Hilb"),
$
$
  X:cal(T) arrow.r.double cal(K)_X,
  quad
  Y:cal(T) arrow.r.double cal(K)_Y.
$

#object-entry(elements.natural_contraction_feasibility)

The desired successor passage is then one natural transformation
$
  Gamma:cal(K)_X arrow.r.double cal(K)_Y,
$
not an unrelated contraction selected at every aperture. For each arrow
$a:j arrow.r k$, it owes the square
$
  cal(K)_Y(a) Gamma_j
  =
  Gamma_k cal(K)_X(a).
$
At every object it owes
$
  Gamma_j X_j=Y_j,
  quad
  mat(I,Gamma_j^*;Gamma_j,I) >= 0.
$

For a finite subdiagram these are affine equalities intersected with
positive-semidefinite cones, hence a convex feasibility problem. If every
finite collection of exact constraints is simultaneously feasible,
weak-operator compactness produces a coherent global family. This is not a
license to infer the infinite result from sampled computations: the
arithmetic construction must prove all finite compatibilities or supply a
monotone extension law.

#object-entry(elements.positive_perspective_descent)

This also states exactly what a proof “by perspectives” can mean. Compatible
positive receiver forms descend when they are congruent presentations of the
same anchored response and their transition cocycle closes. Invertible
recharting preserves Hermitian inertia, so no orientation can make a genuine
negative direction positive. The useful convex object is the cone of
compatible positive forms—or, here, the natural contraction feasible set—not the
set of favorable drawings of one indefinite form.

#object-entry(elements.natural_contraction_rh)

This is the same RH wall in a stricter form, not a renamed carrier. The new
