  ]dif r.
$
The unit-modulus boundary supplies equality at $x=0$; it does not determine
the orientation of these interior sweeps.

=== Every pair has a common founding and a reunion

#object-entry(elements.completed_source_common_founding_crossing)

The integer-event expansion now resolves the crossing geometry exactly.
Define
$
  J_(2omega)(n)
  =
  n^(2omega)
  product_(p divides n)(1-p^(-2omega)).
$
Then
$
  w_omega(n)
  =
  frac(J_(2omega)(n),n^(omega+1/2)),
$
and every pair satisfies the meet--join identity
$
  w_omega(m)w_omega(n)
  =
  w_omega(gcd(m,n))
  w_omega(lcm(m,n)).
  quad "(FOUNDING AND REUNION)"
$
The gcd is the actual common divisor-axis standing; the lcm is the
composed reunion label.  Moreover,
$
  gcd(m,n)^(2omega)
  =
  sum_(d divides m,\ d divides n)J_(2omega)(d),
$
so these common axes have an ordinary positive Gram realization.

That does not make each common-founding stratum positive.  Writing
$m=d a$, $n=d b$ with $gcd(a,b)=1$ assigns the crossing to $d$, but the
coprime-arm incidence on ${1,p}$ is
$
  mat(1,&1;1,&0),
$
whose determinant is $-1$.  Möbius inversion gives the exact orientation:
$
  cal(C)_(d,tau)(F)
  =
  sum_(k>=1)
  mu(k)
  norm(
    sum_(j>=1)b_(d k j)^(tau)
  )^2.
  quad "(COMMON-FOUNDING FOIL)"
$
The negative prime layer $\mu(p)=-1$ is not a negative source energy.  It
prevents the $p$-arm's self-interaction—whose founding is $d p$—from being
counted again in the $d$ perspective.  Summation over all $d$ cancels the
Möbius layers and reconstitutes the one complete causal current.  Therefore
the gcd is a valid crossing coordinate but not an independently shortable
positive constituent.

The same inseparability occurs between arithmetic admission and the Gamma
response.  On every finite cut,
$
  cal(V)=cal(A)cal(G),
$
and hence
$
  I-cal(V)^*cal(V)
  =
  (
    I-cal(G)^*cal(G)
  )
  -
  cal(G)^*
  (
    cal(A)^*cal(A)-I
  )
  cal(G).
  quad "(COMPLETED CAPACITANCE)"
$
This is the precise storage-versus-load relation.  Neither side may be
assigned a sign before their coupling.

The load is not merely formal.  Once $tau>log 2$, a sufficiently narrow
nonnegative pulse has disjoint $n=1$ and $n=2$ images, so
$
  norm(cal(A)_(omega,tau)G)^2
  >=
  (1+w_omega(2)^2)norm(G)^2
  >
  norm(G)^2.
$
Arithmetic admission is already expansive at the first nontrivial event;
the completed response must carry the compensating storage.

There is a concrete reason.  The arithmetic transfer is
$
  hat(A)_omega(p)
  =
  frac(
    zeta(p+1/2-omega),
    zeta(p+1/2+omega)
  ),
$
while the completed transfer is
$
  hat(psi)_omega(p)
  =
  frac(Xi(p-omega),Xi(p+omega)).
$
For $0<omega<1/2$, the point $p_0=1/2-omega$ lies inside the causal
half-plane.  At $p_0$, $hat(A)_omega$ has a zero caused by the pole
$zeta(1)$, and the separated Gamma/endpoint response has the compensating
pole.  Their product is regular.  The critical value $1/2$ therefore marks
the exact place where a seemingly natural subsystem split becomes
singular: at $omega=1/2$ the cancellation reaches the boundary, and above
it the seam leaves the causal half-plane.

The required construction is consequently not a positive prime-by-prime
or gcd-by-gcd factorization.  It must factor ("COMPLETED CAPACITANCE") with
the Möbius foil and completion seam still co-present.

=== The half-seam retains strict completed capacity

#object-entry(elements.completed_source_seam_regularization)

